  "content": {
    "id": "p4-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Suppose a polynomial has an integer value at every integer. Does the polynomial necessarily have integer coefficients? Prove this if so, or else give a counterexample.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No. For example, $P(x)=\\dfrac{x(x-1)}{2}=\\dfrac12x^2-\\dfrac12x$.\n\nConsecutive integers have an even product, so $P(x)$ is an integer at every integer input.",
      "worked": "No. Consider\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=\\frac{x(x-1)}{2}\\\\P(x)&=\\frac12x^2-\\frac12x.\\end{align*}$$\nFor every integer $x$, one of the consecutive integers $x$ and $x-1$ is even. Hence $x(x-1)$ is even, so $P(x)$ is an integer. Its coefficients $\\frac12$ and $-\\frac12$ are not integers, giving a counterexample.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 32
  },
